@topic _setcursortype
@keys _setcursortype
 _setcursortype

 Selects the cursor shape.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   void _setcursortype(int type);

 type is _NOCURSOR (hidden), _SOLIDCURSOR (block) or _NORMALCURSOR
 (the terminal's default).

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {gotoxy}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic abort
@keys abort
 abort

 Ends the program abnormally.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   void abort(void);

 Raises SIGABRT. If the signal is not caught or ignored, the program
 ends at once without calling the functions registered with atexit;
 buffered output is not flushed.

 Return value
 Does not return.

 See also: {exit} {signal} {assert}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic abs
@keys abs
 abs

 Returns the absolute value of an int.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   int abs(int j);

 The value of abs(INT_MIN) cannot be represented and is undefined.

 Return value
 The absolute value of j.

 See also: {labs} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic acos
@keys acos
 acos

 Arc cosine.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double acos(double x);

 x must be in -1 to 1. The result is in 0 to pi.

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic asctime
@keys asctime
 asctime

 Converts broken-down time to a string.

 Header: time.h

 Syntax
   char *asctime(const struct tm *timeptr);

 The format is "Sun Sep 16 01:03:52 1973\n". A static buffer is used.

 Return value
 A pointer to the string.

 See also: {ctime} {strftime}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic asin
@keys asin
 asin

 Arc sine.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double asin(double x);

 x must be in -1 to 1. The result is in -pi/2 to pi/2.

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic assert
@keys assert
 assert

 Checks a condition while the program runs. (assert.h)

 Header: see description

 Syntax
   void assert(int expression);

 If the expression is 0 a message with the expression, file and line
 is written to stderr and abort is called. Define NDEBUG before
 including assert.h to remove the checks.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {abort}

 Back to {Library functions}

