@topic atan
@keys atan
 atan

 Arc tangent.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double atan(double x);

 The result is in -pi/2 to pi/2.

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic atan2
@keys atan2
 atan2

 Arc tangent of y/x using both signs.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double atan2(double y, double x);

 The result is in -pi to pi and is the angle of the point (x, y).

 Return value
 The angle in radians.

 See also: {atan}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic atexit
@keys atexit
 atexit

 Registers a function to be called when the program ends normally.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   int atexit(void (*func)(void));

 At least 32 functions can be registered. They are called in the
 reverse order of registration by exit and when main returns.

 Return value
 0 on success, nonzero if the function could not be registered.

 See also: {exit} {abort}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic atof
@keys atof
 atof

 Converts a string to a double.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   double atof(const char *nptr);

 Equivalent to strtod(nptr, NULL) except that errors are not reported.

 Return value
 The converted value, or 0 if no conversion is possible.

 See also: {strtod} {atoi}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic atoi
@keys atoi
 atoi

 Converts a string to an int.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   int atoi(const char *nptr);

 Equivalent to (int)strtol(nptr, NULL, 10) except that errors are not
 reported. Leading white space is skipped.

 Return value
 The converted value, or 0 if no conversion is possible.

 See also: {atol} {strtol} {sscanf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic atol
@keys atol
 atol

 Converts a string to a long.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   long atol(const char *nptr);

 Equivalent to strtol(nptr, NULL, 10) except that errors are not
 reported.

 Return value
 The converted value, or 0 if no conversion is possible.

 See also: {atoi} {strtol}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic bios.h
@keys bios.h
 <bios.h>

 The few BIOS-style services that make sense on a terminal.

 Functions

  {biosequip} {bioskey} {biosmemory} {biostime}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic biosequip
@keys biosequip
 biosequip

 Returns the equipment list.

 Header: bios.h

 Syntax
   int biosequip(void);

 A fixed value (0x0021: a display and one floppy-style drive entry) is
 returned; the real hardware is not examined.

 Return value
 The equipment word.

 See also: {biosmemory}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic bioskey
@keys bioskey
 bioskey

 Keyboard services.

 Header: bios.h

 Syntax
   int bioskey(int cmd);

 cmd 0 reads a key and waits (an extended key is returned as scan code
 << 8); cmd 1 tells whether a key is waiting; cmd 2 (shift flags)
 always returns 0.

 Return value
 The key, or the result of the command.

 See also: {getch} {kbhit}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic biosmemory
@keys biosmemory
 biosmemory

 Returns the memory size in kilobytes.

 Header: bios.h

 Syntax
   int biosmemory(void);

 Always 640; the real memory size is not reported.

 Return value
 640.

 See also: {biosequip}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic biostime
@keys biostime
 biostime

 Reads the BIOS clock.

 Header: bios.h

 Syntax
   long biostime(int cmd, long newtime);

 cmd 0 returns the number of clock ticks (18.2 per second) since
 midnight. Setting the clock (cmd 1) is not supported and does
 nothing.

 Return value
 The tick count, or 0 for cmd 1.

 See also: {time} {clock}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic bsearch
@keys bsearch
 bsearch

 Searches a sorted array with a binary search.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   void *bsearch(const void *key, const void *base, size_t nmemb, size_t size, int (*compar)(const void *, const void *));

 The array must be sorted in ascending order according to compar.
 compar(key, element) must return a negative value, zero or a positive
 value when key is less than, equal to or greater than the element.

 Return value
 A pointer to a matching element, or NULL if there is none.

 See also: {qsort}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

static int cmp(const void *a, const void *b)
{
    return *(const int *)a - *(const int *)b;
}

int main(void)
{
    int v[] = { 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 };
    int key = 8;
    int *p = bsearch(&key, v, 5, sizeof v[0], cmp);
    if (p)
        printf("found at index %d\n", (int)(p - v));
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic calloc
@keys calloc
 calloc

 Allocates zero-filled memory for an array.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   void *calloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size);

 The block is nmemb*size bytes long and every byte is 0. Free it with
 free.

 Return value
 A pointer to the block, or NULL if there is not enough memory.

 See also: {malloc} {realloc} {free}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic ceil
@keys ceil
 ceil

 Smallest integer not less than x.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double ceil(double x);

 The result is returned as a double.

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic cgets
@keys cgets
 cgets

 Reads a string from the console.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   char *cgets(char *buf);

 buf[0] must hold the maximum number of characters. The function
 stores the number of characters read in buf[1] and the string, ended
 with a null character, from buf[2]. Backspace edits the line; Enter
 ends it and echoes a newline.

 Return value
 A pointer to buf[2].

 See also: {getch} {cscanf} {getpass}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <conio.h>

int main(void)
{
    char buf[82];
    buf[0] = 80;
    cputs("Name: ");
    cgets(buf);
    cprintf("Hello, %s (%d chars)\r\n", buf + 2, buf[1]);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic clearerr
@keys clearerr
 clearerr

 Clears the end-of-file and error indicators of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   void clearerr(FILE *stream);

 After an error or end of file the indicators of a stream stay set
 until they are cleared with clearerr, rewind or by reopening the
 stream.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {feof} {ferror} {rewind}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic clock
@keys clock
 clock

 Returns the processor time used by the program.

 Header: time.h

 Syntax
   clock_t clock(void);

 Divide by CLOCKS_PER_SEC to get seconds.

 Return value
 The time, or (clock_t)-1 if not available.

 See also: {time} {difftime}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic clock_t
@keys clock_t
 clock_t

 Arithmetic type for processor times; CLOCKS_PER_SEC gives the units
 per second.

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic clreol
@keys clreol
 clreol

 Clears from the cursor to the end of the line in the text window.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   void clreol(void);

 The cursor does not move.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {clrscr} {delline}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic clrscr
@keys clrscr
 clrscr

 Clears the text window and moves the cursor to its top left corner.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   void clrscr(void);

 Only the current window (see window) is cleared.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {window} {clreol} {gotoxy}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic conio.h
@keys conio.h
 <conio.h>

 Console input and output: text windows, colours, cursor control and
 unbuffered keyboard input.

 Functions

  {_setcursortype} {cgets} {clreol} {clrscr} {cprintf} {cputs} {cscanf}
