@topic cos
@keys cos
 cos

 Cosine of an angle in radians.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double cos(double x);

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic cosh
@keys cosh
 cosh

 Hyperbolic cosine.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double cosh(double x);

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic cprintf
@keys cprintf
 cprintf

 Writes formatted output to the text window.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int cprintf(const char *fmt, ...);

 Same conversions as printf (see {printf formats}), but the output
 goes to the console using the current colours and window. A newline
 moves down only; use \r\n to start a new line at the left edge.

 Return value
 The number of characters written.

 See also: {cputs} {putch} {printf}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <conio.h>

int main(void)
{
    clrscr();
    textcolor(YELLOW);
    textbackground(BLUE);
    gotoxy(10, 5);
    cprintf("Value: %d", 42);
    getch();
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic cputs
@keys cputs
 cputs

 Writes a string to the text window.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int cputs(const char *s);

 No newline is added. Uses the current colours and window.

 Return value
 The last character written.

 See also: {cprintf} {putch} {puts}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic cscanf
@keys cscanf
 cscanf

 Reads formatted input from the console.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int cscanf(const char *fmt, ...);

 Reads a line with cgets (echoed) and converts it as scanf does. See
 {scanf formats}.

 Return value
 The number of items assigned.

 See also: {cgets} {scanf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic ctime
@keys ctime
 ctime

 Converts a calendar time to a string.

 Header: time.h

 Syntax
   char *ctime(const time_t *timer);

 Same as asctime(localtime(timer)).

 Return value
 A pointer to the string.

 See also: {asctime} {localtime}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic ctype.h
@keys ctype.h
 <ctype.h>

 Character classification and case conversion. Arguments are unsigned
 char values or EOF.

 Functions

  {isalnum} {isalpha} {iscntrl} {isdigit} {isgraph} {islower} {isprint}
  {ispunct} {isspace} {isupper} {isxdigit} {tolower} {toupper}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic delay
@keys delay
 delay

 Waits for a number of milliseconds.

 Header: dos.h

 Syntax
   void delay(unsigned milliseconds);

 Uses the system sleep, so the processor is not kept busy.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {sleep} {sound}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <dos.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i;
    for (i = 3; i > 0; i--) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
        delay(1000);
    }
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic delline
@keys delline
 delline

 Deletes the line with the cursor.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   void delline(void);

 Lines below move up and a blank line appears at the bottom of the
 window.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {insline} {clreol}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic difftime
@keys difftime
 difftime

 Difference of two times in seconds.

 Header: time.h

 Syntax
   double difftime(time_t time1, time_t time0);

 Return value
 time1 - time0 as a double.

 See also: {time}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic directvideo
@keys directvideo
 directvideo

 int directvideo. Kept for compatibility. Console output always goes
 through the terminal, so the value has no effect.

