@keys dos.h
 <dos.h>

 DOS-style date, time and delay functions and a few system calls.

 Functions

  {delay} {dostounix} {getdate} {getdfree} {getpid} {gettime} {nosound}
  {setdate} {settime} {sleep} {sound} {unixtodos} {unlink}

 Types, macros and objects

  {struct date} {struct time} {struct dfree}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic dostounix
@keys dostounix
 dostounix

 Converts DOS date and time to calendar time.

 Header: dos.h

 Syntax
   long dostounix(struct date *d, struct time *t);

 The values are taken as local time. Gives seconds since 1 January
 1970 UTC.

 Return value
 The calendar time.

 See also: {unixtodos} {getdate} {gettime} {mktime}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic errno
@keys errno
 errno

 Math functions report domain errors with EDOM and range errors with
 ERANGE in errno.

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic exit
@keys exit
 exit

 Ends the program normally.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   void exit(int status);

 Functions registered with atexit are called in reverse order, open
 streams are flushed and closed, and the program ends with status.
 EXIT_SUCCESS and 0 mean success, EXIT_FAILURE means failure.

 Return value
 Does not return.

 See also: {atexit} {abort}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic exp
@keys exp
 exp

 e raised to the power x.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double exp(double x);

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {log} {floor} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fabs
@keys fabs
 fabs

 Absolute value of a double.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double fabs(double x);

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {floor} {ceil}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fclose
@keys fclose
 fclose

 Closes a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fclose(FILE *stream);

 Any buffered output is written, any unread buffered input is
 discarded and the stream is closed. A buffer that the library
 allocated is freed. The stream pointer is not valid after the call.

 Return value
 0 on success, EOF if an error occurred.

 See also: {fopen} {fflush} {freopen}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic feof
@keys feof
 feof

 Tests the end-of-file indicator of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int feof(FILE *stream);

 The indicator is set by a read operation that hit the end of the
 file, not by reaching the last byte. Test the result of the read call
 itself first and use feof only to tell end of file from an error.

 Return value
 Nonzero if the end-of-file indicator is set, otherwise 0.

 See also: {ferror} {clearerr} {fgetc}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic ferror
@keys ferror
 ferror

 Tests the error indicator of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int ferror(FILE *stream);

 The indicator stays set until clearerr, rewind or fclose.

 Return value
 Nonzero if an error occurred on the stream, otherwise 0.

 See also: {feof} {clearerr} {perror}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fflush
@keys fflush
 fflush

 Writes buffered output of a stream to the file.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fflush(FILE *stream);

 If stream is NULL, all streams that are open for output are flushed.
 The effect on an input stream is undefined.

 Return value
 0 on success, EOF on a write error.

 See also: {setvbuf} {fclose}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fgetc
@keys fgetc
 fgetc

 Reads the next character from a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fgetc(FILE *stream);

 The character is read as an unsigned char and converted to int.

 Return value
 The character, or EOF at end of file or on error.

 See also: {getc} {getchar} {ungetc} {fputc}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fgetpos
@keys fgetpos
 fgetpos

 Stores the current file position of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fgetpos(FILE *stream, fpos_t *pos);

 The value stored in pos is meant only for fsetpos; do not interpret
 it.

 Return value
 0 on success, nonzero on error (errno is set).

 See also: {fsetpos} {ftell} {fseek}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fgets
@keys fgets
 fgets

 Reads a line from a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   char *fgets(char *s, int n, FILE *stream);

 Reads at most n-1 characters, stopping after a newline, which is
 stored in the string. The string is always ended with a null
 character.

 Return value
 s, or NULL at end of file with nothing read, or on error.

 See also: {gets} {fputs} {fgetc}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    char line[80];
    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin))
        fputs(line, stdout);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic float.h
@keys float.h
 float.h

 Characteristics of floating types: FLT_/DBL_/LDBL_ with DIG, EPSILON,
 MANT_DIG, MAX, MAX_EXP, MIN, MIN_EXP and so on, FLT_RADIX,
 FLT_ROUNDS.

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic floor
@keys floor
 floor

 Largest integer not greater than x.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double floor(double x);

 The result is returned as a double.

 Return value
 The result. A domain error sets errno to EDOM; a range error sets it
 to ERANGE.

 See also: {sin} {cos} {tan} {atan2} {sqrt} {pow} {exp} {log} {ceil} {fabs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fmod
@keys fmod
 fmod

 Floating-point remainder of x/y.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double fmod(double x, double y);

 The result has the sign of x and a magnitude less than that of y.

 Return value
 The remainder; 0 if y is 0 (domain error).

 See also: {modf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fopen
@keys fopen
 fopen

 Opens a file and returns a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *mode);

 The mode starts with one of: r (read), w (create or truncate for
 writing), a (append). A + after it opens the file for update (reading
 and writing); a b means a binary file, which on this system is the
 same as text. When a file is opened for update, switch between
 reading and writing with fflush, fseek, fsetpos or rewind.

 Return value
 A stream pointer, or NULL if the file could not be opened (errno is
 set).

 See also: {fclose} {freopen} {tmpfile} {setvbuf}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    FILE *f = fopen("data.txt", "w");
    if (f == NULL) {
        perror("data.txt");
        return 1;
    }
    fprintf(f, "hello\n");
    fclose(f);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic fpos_t
@keys fpos_t
 fpos_t

 A type that holds a file position, used with fgetpos and fsetpos.

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fprintf
@keys fprintf
 fprintf

 Writes formatted output to a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...);

 The format string contains ordinary characters, which are copied, and
 conversion specifications that start with %. See {printf formats} for
 every conversion.

 Return value
 The number of characters written, or a negative value on error.

 See also: {printf} {sprintf} {vfprintf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fputc
@keys fputc
 fputc

 Writes a character to a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fputc(int c, FILE *stream);

 The character is converted to unsigned char first.

 Return value
 The character written, or EOF on error.

 See also: {putc} {putchar} {fgetc}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fputs
@keys fputs
 fputs

 Writes a string to a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fputs(const char *s, FILE *stream);

 The terminating null character is not written, and no newline is
 added.

 Return value
 A nonnegative value, or EOF on error.

 See also: {puts} {fgets} {fprintf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fread
@keys fread
 fread

 Reads blocks of data from a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   size_t fread(void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE *stream);

 Reads up to nmemb items of size bytes each into the array at ptr. A
 partial item at the end of the file is lost.

 Return value
 The number of complete items read; it is less than nmemb at end of
 file or on error (use feof or ferror to tell).

 See also: {fwrite} {fgets} {fopen}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int v[4];
    FILE *f = fopen("nums.bin", "rb");
    if (f != NULL) {
        size_t n = fread(v, sizeof v[0], 4, f);
        printf("%d items\n", (int)n);
        fclose(f);
    }
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic free
@keys free
 free

 Releases memory.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   void free(void *ptr);

 ptr must be NULL or a pointer returned by malloc, calloc or realloc
 that has not been freed. Freeing NULL does nothing.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {malloc} {calloc} {realloc}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic freopen
@keys freopen
 freopen

 Reopens a stream on another file.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   FILE *freopen(const char *filename, const char *mode, FILE *stream);

 The file that stream was associated with is closed first (errors are
 ignored). It is typically used to redirect stdin, stdout or stderr.

 Return value
 stream, or NULL if the file could not be opened.

 See also: {fopen} {fclose}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic frexp
@keys frexp
 frexp

 Splits a number into fraction and power of two.

 Header: math.h

 Syntax
   double frexp(double value, int *exp);

 value = fraction * 2^exp, with the fraction in 0.5 to 1 (or 0).

 Return value
 The fraction; the exponent is stored in *exp.

 See also: {ldexp} {modf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fscanf
@keys fscanf
 fscanf

 Reads formatted input from a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fscanf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...);

 Works like scanf but reads from stream. See {scanf formats}.

 Return value
 The number of items assigned, or EOF if input ended before the first
 conversion.

 See also: {scanf} {sscanf} {fgets}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fseek
@keys fseek
 fseek

 Moves the file position of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int whence);

 whence is SEEK_SET (from the start), SEEK_CUR (from the current
 position) or SEEK_END (from the end of the file). A successful call
 clears the end-of-file indicator and cancels the effect of ungetc.

 Return value
 0 on success, nonzero on error.

 See also: {ftell} {rewind} {fsetpos}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fsetpos
@keys fsetpos
 fsetpos

 Restores a file position saved by fgetpos.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int fsetpos(FILE *stream, const fpos_t *pos);

 Return value
 0 on success, nonzero on error.

 See also: {fgetpos} {fseek}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic ftell
@keys ftell
 ftell

 Returns the current file position of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   long ftell(FILE *stream);

 For a binary file it is the number of bytes from the start of the
 file.

 Return value
 The position, or -1L on error.

 See also: {fseek} {fgetpos}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic fwrite
@keys fwrite
 fwrite

 Writes blocks of data to a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   size_t fwrite(const void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE *stream);

 Writes nmemb items of size bytes each from the array at ptr.

 Return value
 The number of items written; less than nmemb only on error.

 See also: {fread} {fputs} {fopen}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getc
@keys getc
 getc

 Reads the next character from a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int getc(FILE *stream);

 Same as fgetc; it may be implemented as a macro, so the argument must
 not have side effects.

 Return value
 The character, or EOF at end of file or on error.

 See also: {fgetc} {getchar} {ungetc}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getch
@keys getch
 getch

 Reads a key without echo.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int getch(void);

 Waits for a key. Extended keys (function keys, cursor keys) return 0
 first, and the next call returns a scan code. The terminal is
 switched to raw mode while reading.

 Return value
 The character code.

 See also: {getche} {kbhit} {ungetch}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <conio.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int c;
    cputs("Press a key: ");
    c = getch();
    if (c == 0)
        printf("extended key, scan code %d\n", getch());
    else
        printf("you pressed %c\n", c);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic getchar
@keys getchar
 getchar

 Reads the next character from stdin.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int getchar(void);

 Same as getc(stdin).

 Return value
 The character, or EOF.

 See also: {getc} {gets} {scanf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getche
@keys getche
 getche

 Reads a key and echoes it.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int getche(void);

 Same as getch, but a printable character is written to the window.

 Return value
 The character code.

 See also: {getch} {kbhit}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getdate
@keys getdate
 getdate

 Gets the current system date.

 Header: dos.h

 Syntax
   void getdate(struct date *d);

 Fills in da_year, da_mon (1-12) and da_day (1-31) of {struct date}.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {gettime} {setdate}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getdfree
@keys getdfree
 getdfree

 Gets the free space of the disk.

 Header: dos.h

 Syntax
   void getdfree(unsigned char drive, struct dfree *table);

 Drive letters do not exist on this system: the argument is ignored
 and the file system that holds / is described. The counts are limited
 to 65535 (the structure is {struct dfree}).

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {dostounix}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getenv
@keys getenv
 getenv

 Gets the value of an environment variable.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   char *getenv(const char *name);

 The returned string must not be modified and may be overwritten by
 later calls.

 Return value
 The value, or NULL if the variable is not set.

 See also: {system}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getpass
@keys getpass
 getpass

 Reads a password without echo.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   char *getpass(const char *prompt);

 The prompt is written first; the text is read until Enter. The result
 is in a static buffer of 127 characters.

 Return value
 A pointer to the password.

 See also: {cgets} {getch}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic getpid
@keys getpid
 getpid

 Returns the process id of the program.

 Header: dos.h

 Syntax
   int getpid(void);

 Return value
 The process id.

 See also: {system}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic gets
@keys gets
 gets

 Reads a line from stdin.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   char *gets(char *s);

 The newline is removed. The function cannot limit the length of the
