@topic printf
@keys printf
 printf

 Writes formatted output to stdout.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int printf(const char *format, ...);

 Characters of format are copied to the output, except conversion
 specifications that start with %, which format the following
 arguments. See {printf formats} for the flags, width, precision,
 length modifiers and conversions.

 Return value
 The number of characters written, or a negative value on error.

 See also: {fprintf} {sprintf} {vprintf} {puts}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n = 42;
    double x = 3.14159;
    printf("%5d|%-5d|%05d\n", n, n, n);
    printf("%8.3f|%e|%g\n", x, x, x);
    printf("%s has %d chars\n", "text", 4);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic printf formats
@keys format specifier conversion %d %s %f
@alias format specifiers
 printf formats

 A conversion specification has the form

   %[flags][width][.precision][length]conversion

 Flags
   -    left-justify in the field
   +    always print a sign
   space  a space in front of a positive number
   #    alternate form: 0 before octal, 0x before hex, always a
        decimal point for floating types
   0    pad with zeros instead of spaces

 Width and precision are numbers or * (taken from the arguments).
 Precision: minimum digits for integers, digits after the point for
 e, E and f, significant digits for g and G, maximum characters for s.

 Length: h (short), l (long, or wide for c and s), L (long double)

 Conversions
   d i  signed decimal        o  octal          u  unsigned decimal
   x X  hexadecimal           c  character      s  string
   f    fixed point           e E  exponent     g G  shortest of e/f
   p    pointer               n  stores the count so far
   %    a percent sign

 See {printf}, {fprintf}, {sprintf}, {scanf formats}

