@topic rename
@keys rename
 rename

 Renames a file.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int rename(const char *old, const char *new);

 If a file with the new name exists it is replaced.

 Return value
 0 on success, nonzero on error.

 See also: {remove}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic rewind
@keys rewind
 rewind

 Sets the file position to the start of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   void rewind(FILE *stream);

 Equivalent to fseek(stream, 0L, SEEK_SET) followed by
 clearerr(stream).

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {fseek} {clearerr}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic scanf
@keys scanf
 scanf

 Reads formatted input from stdin.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int scanf(const char *format, ...);

 The format string holds conversion specifications that start with %,
 white space (which matches any amount of white space) and ordinary
 characters (which must match the input). Every argument after the
 format must be a pointer. See {scanf formats}.

 Return value
 The number of items assigned, or EOF if input ended before the first
 conversion.

 See also: {fscanf} {sscanf} {gets}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a;
    float b;
    char name[20];
    if (scanf("%d %f %19s", &a, &b, name) == 3)
        printf("%d %g %s\n", a, b, name);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic scanf formats
@keys scanf conversion input
 scanf formats

 A conversion specification has the form

   %[*][width][length]conversion

 * suppresses assignment. White space in the format matches any amount
 of white space in the input; other characters must match exactly.

 Conversions
   d    decimal int            i  int, base from prefix
   o    octal                  u  unsigned decimal
   x X  hexadecimal            c  characters (no skip of white space)
   s    word (stops at white space)
   e f g  floating point       p  pointer
   n    characters read so far
   [set]  characters from the set; [^set] characters not in it
   %    a percent sign

 Length h (short), l (long, or double for floating types), L (long double).
 Arguments must be pointers. See {scanf}, {sscanf}, {fscanf}, {printf formats}

@topic setbuf
@keys setbuf
 setbuf

 Sets the buffer of a stream.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   void setbuf(FILE *stream, char *buf);

 buf must point to an array of BUFSIZ characters; with NULL the stream
 is unbuffered. Call it before any other operation on the stream.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {setvbuf}

 Back to {Library functions}

