@topic strcat
@keys strcat
 strcat

 Appends a string.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strcat(char *s1, const char *s2);

 The first character of s2 replaces the null character that ends s1.
 The array s1 must be large enough for both strings and the new null
 character.

 Return value
 s1.

 See also: {strncat} {strcpy}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strchr
@keys strchr
 strchr

 Finds the first occurrence of a character in a string.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strchr(const char *s, int c);

 The terminating null character is part of the string, so strchr(s,
 '\0') returns a pointer to it.

 Return value
 A pointer to the character, or NULL if it is not found.

 See also: {strrchr} {strpbrk} {memchr}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strcmp
@keys strcmp
 strcmp

 Compares two strings.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2);

 The characters are compared as unsigned char values.

 Return value
 Negative, zero or positive if s1 is less than, equal to or greater
 than s2.

 See also: {strncmp} {strcoll} {memcmp}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strcoll
@keys strcoll
 strcoll

 Compares two strings using the current locale.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   int strcoll(const char *s1, const char *s2);

 In the "C" locale it is the same as strcmp.

 Return value
 Negative, zero or positive, like strcmp.

 See also: {strcmp} {strxfrm} {setlocale}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strcpy
@keys strcpy
 strcpy

 Copies a string.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strcpy(char *s1, const char *s2);

 Copies s2 including the null character to s1. The array s1 must be
 large enough and must not overlap s2.

 Return value
 s1.

 See also: {strncpy} {memcpy} {strcat}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strcspn
@keys strcspn
 strcspn

 Counts the characters at the start of a string that are not in a set.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   size_t strcspn(const char *s1, const char *s2);

 Return value
 The length of the initial part of s1 that contains no character of
 s2.

 See also: {strspn} {strpbrk}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strerror
@keys strerror
 strerror

 Returns the text of an error number.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strerror(int errnum);

 The string must not be modified and may be overwritten by later
 calls.

 Return value
 A pointer to the message.

 See also: {perror} {errno}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strftime
@keys strftime
 strftime

 Formats a time as text.

 Header: time.h

 Syntax
   size_t strftime(char *s, size_t maxsize, const char *format, const struct tm *timeptr);

 Conversions: %a %A weekday, %b %B month, %c date and time, %d day, %H
 %I hour, %j day of year, %m month, %M minute, %p AM/PM, %S second, %U
 %W week, %w weekday number, %x date, %X time, %y %Y year, %Z zone, %%
 percent sign.

 Return value
 The number of characters stored (not counting the null), or 0 if
 maxsize is too small.

 See also: {asctime} {localtime}

 Back to {Library functions}

