@topic strncat
@keys strncat
 strncat

 Appends at most n characters of a string.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strncat(char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);

 Always appends a null character; unlike strncpy it never pads.

 Return value
 s1.

 See also: {strcat} {strncpy}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strncmp
@keys strncmp
 strncmp

 Compares at most n characters of two strings.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   int strncmp(const char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);

 Return value
 Negative, zero or positive, like strcmp.

 See also: {strcmp} {memcmp}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strncpy
@keys strncpy
 strncpy

 Copies at most n characters of a string.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strncpy(char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);

 If s2 is shorter than n, the rest of s1 is filled with null
 characters. If s2 has n or more characters, s1 is not null-
 terminated.

 Return value
 s1.

 See also: {strcpy} {strncat}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strpbrk
@keys strpbrk
 strpbrk

 Finds the first character of a string that is in a set.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strpbrk(const char *s1, const char *s2);

 Return value
 A pointer to the character, or NULL.

 See also: {strchr} {strcspn}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strrchr
@keys strrchr
 strrchr

 Finds the last occurrence of a character in a string.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strrchr(const char *s, int c);

 Return value
 A pointer to the character, or NULL if it is not found.

 See also: {strchr}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strspn
@keys strspn
 strspn

 Counts the characters at the start of a string that are in a set.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   size_t strspn(const char *s1, const char *s2);

 Return value
 The length of the initial part of s1 that consists only of characters
 of s2.

 See also: {strcspn}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strstr
@keys strstr
 strstr

 Finds a substring.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strstr(const char *s1, const char *s2);

 Return value
 A pointer to the first occurrence of s2 in s1, s1 if s2 is empty, or
 NULL.

 See also: {strchr}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strtod
@keys strtod
 strtod

 Converts the start of a string to a double.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   double strtod(const char *nptr, char **endptr);

 Skips leading white space, then reads an optional sign, digits with
 an optional decimal point and an optional exponent. If endptr is not
 NULL it receives a pointer to the first unconverted character.

 Return value
 The value. If the value is out of range, HUGE_VAL (with the sign) is
 returned or 0 for underflow and errno is set to ERANGE.

 See also: {atof} {strtol}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strtok
@keys strtok
 strtok

 Splits a string into tokens.

 Header: string.h

 Syntax
   char *strtok(char *s1, const char *s2);

 The first call passes the string, later calls pass NULL. s2 lists the
 delimiter characters and may differ from call to call. The string is
 modified (delimiters are replaced with null characters), and the
 function keeps internal state, so it is not reentrant.

 Return value
 A pointer to the next token, or NULL when there are no more.

 See also: {strchr} {strspn}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    char text[] = "one, two;three";
    char *tok = strtok(text, ", ;");
    while (tok != NULL) {
        puts(tok);
        tok = strtok(NULL, ", ;");
    }
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic strtol
@keys strtol
 strtol

 Converts the start of a string to a long.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   long strtol(const char *nptr, char **endptr, int base);

 base is 2 to 36, or 0 to accept a 0x prefix for hexadecimal and a
 leading 0 for octal. Leading white space and a sign are accepted. If
 endptr is not NULL it receives a pointer to the first unconverted
 character.

 Return value
 The value. On overflow LONG_MAX or LONG_MIN is returned and errno is
 set to ERANGE.

 See also: {strtoul} {atol} {atoi}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic strtoul
@keys strtoul
 strtoul

 Converts the start of a string to an unsigned long.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   unsigned long strtoul(const char *nptr, char **endptr, int base);

 Same as strtol except for the result type.

 Return value
 The value. On overflow ULONG_MAX is returned and errno is set to
 ERANGE.

 See also: {strtol}

 Back to {Library functions}

