 Back to {Library functions}

@topic va_arg
@keys va_arg
 va_arg

 Gets the next variable argument. (stdarg.h)

 Header: see description

 Syntax
   type va_arg(va_list ap, type);

 Return value
 The value, of the given type.

 See also: {va_start} {va_end}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic va_end
@keys va_end
 va_end

 Ends the use of a variable argument list. (stdarg.h)

 Header: see description

 Syntax
   void va_end(va_list ap);

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {va_start}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic va_start
@keys va_start
 va_start

 Starts a variable argument list. (stdarg.h)

 Header: see description

 Syntax
   void va_start(va_list ap, parmN);

 parmN is the last named parameter of the function.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {va_arg} {va_end} {vprintf}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static int sum(int count, ...)
{
    va_list ap;
    int total = 0;
    va_start(ap, count);
    while (count-- > 0)
        total += va_arg(ap, int);
    va_end(ap);
    return total;
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", sum(3, 10, 20, 30));
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic vfprintf
@keys vfprintf
 vfprintf

 Writes formatted output to a stream using an argument list.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int vfprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, va_list arg);

 Like fprintf, but the variable arguments are passed as a va_list. The
 function does not call va_end.

 Return value
 The number of characters written, or a negative value on error.

 See also: {fprintf} {vprintf} {vsprintf} {va_start}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic vprintf
@keys vprintf
 vprintf

 Writes formatted output to stdout using an argument list.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int vprintf(const char *format, va_list arg);

 Like printf with a va_list. The function does not call va_end.

 Return value
 The number of characters written, or a negative value on error.

 See also: {printf} {vfprintf} {va_start}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <stdio.h>

void log_msg(const char *fmt, ...)
{
    va_list ap;
    va_start(ap, fmt);
    vprintf(fmt, ap);
    va_end(ap);
}

int main(void)
{
    log_msg("%s=%d\n", "x", 5);
    return 0;
}
@end

@topic vsprintf
@keys vsprintf
 vsprintf

 Writes formatted output to a string using an argument list.

 Header: stdio.h

 Syntax
   int vsprintf(char *s, const char *format, va_list arg);

 Like sprintf with a va_list. The array must be large enough.

 Return value
 The number of characters stored, not counting the null character.

 See also: {sprintf} {vprintf}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic wcstombs
@keys wcstombs
 wcstombs

 Converts a wide-character string to a multibyte string.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   size_t wcstombs(char *s, const wchar_t *pwcs, size_t n);

 Return value
 The number of bytes stored (not counting the null), or (size_t)-1.

 See also: {mbstowcs} {wctomb}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic wctomb
@keys wctomb
 wctomb

 Converts a wide character to a multibyte character.

 Header: stdlib.h

 Syntax
   int wctomb(char *s, wchar_t wchar);

 Return value
 The number of bytes stored, or -1.

 See also: {mbtowc} {wcstombs}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic wherex
@keys wherex
 wherex

 Returns the column of the cursor in the text window.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int wherex(void);

 Counted from 1.

 Return value
 The column.

 See also: {wherey} {gotoxy}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic wherey
@keys wherey
 wherey

 Returns the row of the cursor in the text window.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   int wherey(void);

 Counted from 1.

 Return value
 The row.

 See also: {wherex} {gotoxy}

 Back to {Library functions}

@topic window
@keys window
 window

 Defines the active text window.

 Header: conio.h

 Syntax
   void window(int left, int top, int right, int bottom);

 The corners are screen coordinates counted from 1. If they are
 invalid, the call is ignored. Output with cprintf, cputs, putch and
 clrscr is limited to the window, and the cursor moves to its top left
 corner.

 Return value
 Nothing.

 See also: {gotoxy} {clrscr} {gettextinfo}

 Back to {Library functions}

 Example

@example
#include <conio.h>

int main(void)
{
    textbackground(BLUE);
    window(10, 5, 50, 15);
    clrscr();
    cputs("Inside the window\r\n");
    getch();
    return 0;
}
@end
